#{[17592186338825 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨-1,1,-2⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨1,1,3⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,1,3⟩ & =⟨-1,1,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,3⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,1,3⟩ & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,3⟩ & =-6 \\\\ x+y+3z & =-6 \\\\ x+y+3z+6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338826 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨2,3,-1⟩.$ From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨3,2,-2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=⟨3,2,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨3,2,-2⟩ & =⟨2,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,2,-2⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨3,2,-2⟩ & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,2,-2⟩ & =14 \\\\ 3x+2y-2z & =14 \\\\ 3x+2y-2z-14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338827 "We need a vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,-2,1⟩=10$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}=⟨1,-2,1⟩.$ So, $\\mathbf{v}⊥\\mathbf{n}$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-2,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ {t}_{1}-2{t}_{2}+{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{3} & =2{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}_{1} \\\\ {t}_{2} \\\\ 2{t}_{2}-{t}_{1}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $\\mathbf{v}$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{2}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{2}=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{1},\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186338828 "We need a vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨3,0,1⟩=5$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}=⟨3,0,1⟩.$ So, $\\mathbf{v}⊥\\mathbf{n}$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,0,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3{t}_{1}+{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{3} & =-3{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},-3{t}_{1}⟩\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).$ \n\nWe are told that the second component of $\\mathbf{v}$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{2}=0.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{1}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{1}=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=⟨\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{1},\\hspace{0.167em}0,\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{-3}⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{c}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [17592186338829 "We need a vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-5,-1,4⟩=6$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}=⟨-5,-1,4⟩.$ So, $\\mathbf{v}⊥\\mathbf{n}$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,-1,4⟩ & =0 \\\\ -5{t}_{1}-{t}_{2}+4{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =-5{t}_{1}+4{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=⟨{t}_{1},-5{t}_{1}+4{t}_{3},{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).$ \n\nWe are told that the third component of $\\mathbf{v}$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{3}=0.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{1}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{1}=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=⟨\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{1},\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{-5},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{b}=-\\frac{1}{5}.$"] [17592186338830 "We need a vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-2,2,-4⟩=15$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}=⟨-2,2,-4⟩.$ So, $\\mathbf{v}⊥\\mathbf{n}$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,2,-4⟩ & =0 \\\\ -2{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}-4{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ 2{t}_{2} & =2{t}_{1}+4{t}_{3} \\\\ {t}_{2} & ={t}_{1}+2{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=⟨{t}_{1},\\hspace{0.167em}{t}_{1}+2{t}_{3},\\hspace{0.167em}{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $\\mathbf{v}$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{3}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{3}=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{2},\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{1}⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{b}{c}=2.$"] [17592186338831 "We need a vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-1,1,-3⟩=8$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}=⟨-1,1,-3⟩.$ So, $\\mathbf{v}⊥\\mathbf{n}$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,1,-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ -{t}_{1}+{t}_{2}-3{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & ={t}_{1}+3{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{1}+3{t}_{3},{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).$ \n\nWe are told that the third component of $\\mathbf{v}$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{3}=0.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{1}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{1}=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=⟨\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{1},\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{1},\\hspace{0.167em}0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{b}=1.$"] [17592186338833 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨2,0,-3⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & ={\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}\\times {\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨2,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ & =⟨2,0,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ & =4 \\\\ 2x+3y & =4 \\\\ 2x+3y-4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338842 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨4,-2,3⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(-1,0,1)$ and $B(1,0,-1)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,0,-1⟩-⟨-1,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,0,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨2,0,-2⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨2,0,-2⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,-2⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨2,0,-2⟩ & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338843 "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $\\mathbf{q}=⟨2,1,-3⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1+t \\\\ y &=2-t \\\\ z &=3+2t\\end{aligned}\\right.\\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨1,2,3⟩+t\\text{ }\\underbrace{⟨1,-1,2⟩}_{\\mathbf{v}}.\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $\\mathbf{v}=⟨1,-1,2⟩.$ So, a normal  vector to the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\mathbf{v}=⟨1,-1,2⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{q})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩ & =⟨2,1,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,-1,2⟩ & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338858 "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $Q$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨-1,0,4⟩$ and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=⟨2,-4,1⟩$  and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=⟨0,-1,\\sqrt{3}⟩.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=⟨-1,0,4⟩+s⟨2,-4,1⟩+t⟨0,-1,\\sqrt{3}⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\n$$"] [17592186338859 "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $S$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$, and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$  and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+s\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\n$$"] [17592186338860 "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $T$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$, and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$  and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0.25\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+s\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0.25\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\n$$"] [17592186338861 "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+s\\mathbf{b}+t\\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $M$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$, and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$  and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\begin{bmatrix}-14 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+s\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{1em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\n$$"] [17592186338862 "From the parametric equation, we have that $\\mathbf{v}=⟨0,-1,3⟩$ and $\\mathbf{w}=⟨1,2,-1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{v}\\times \\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1\\end{array}} \\\\  & =-5\\mathbf{i}+3\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-5,3,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨-5,3,1⟩.$"] [17592186338863 "From the parametric equation, we have that $\\mathbf{v}=⟨3,0,4⟩$ and $\\mathbf{w}=⟨1,-1,1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{v}\\times \\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{array}} \\\\  & =4\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}-3\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨4,1,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨4,1,-3⟩.$"] [17592186338864 "The direction vectors of the lines are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=⟨1,2,-3⟩$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=⟨-1,0,3⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $C(1,\\sqrt{2},-1)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $\\mathbf{c}=⟨1,\\sqrt{2},-1⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{c}+s{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+t{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨1,\\sqrt{2},-1⟩+s⟨1,2,-3⟩+t⟨-1,0,3⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338865 "The direction vectors of the lines are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=⟨4,-1,5⟩$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=⟨0,2,3⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $D(6,2,1)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $\\mathbf{d}=⟨6,2,1⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{d}+s{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+t{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨6,2,1⟩+s⟨4,-1,5⟩+t⟨0,2,3⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338866 "The direction vectors of the lines are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=⟨5,1,-2⟩$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=⟨1,11,3⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $E(16,-1,3)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $\\mathbf{e}=⟨16,-1,3⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{e}+s{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+t{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨16,-1,3⟩+s⟨5,1,-2⟩+t⟨1,11,3⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338879 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{LM}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{LN},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{LM} & =⟨2,5,-3⟩-⟨-1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,6,-4⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{LN} & =⟨0,-8,-1⟩-⟨-1,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-7,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $\\mathbf{l}=⟨-1,-1,1⟩$ of the point $l,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{l}+s\\overset{\\to }{LM}+t\\overset{\\to }{LN} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨-1,-1,1⟩+s⟨3,6,-4⟩+t⟨1,-7,-2⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186338881 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{UV}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{UW},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =⟨3,4,-3⟩-⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,5,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{UW} & =⟨2,0,0⟩-⟨2,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $\\mathbf{u}=⟨2,-1,0⟩$ of the point $U,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{u}+s\\overset{\\to }{UV}+t\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨2,-1,0⟩+s⟨1,5,-3⟩+t⟨0,1,0⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186342188 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $\\mathbf{0}=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ since ${0}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}\\ne 1.$\n\n- The set $B$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under vector addition. Indeed, both $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,0,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨0,1,1⟩$ belong to $B$ but \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,0,1⟩+⟨0,1,1⟩=⟨1,1,2⟩\n$$\n\n \ndoes not satisfy the condition $xyz=0$ since $1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\ne 0.$\n\n- The set $C$ is not a subspace because it does not contain the zero vector. Indeed, $\\mathbf{0}=⟨0,0,0⟩$ does not satisfy the condition $y=-1$ since $0\\ne -1.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None.\""] [17592186363024 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $\\mathrm{Π}$ is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨3,5,0⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+5y+15 & =0 \\\\ 5y & =-3x-15 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{5}x-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$, we get $y=-3.$ Hence, the point $P(0,-3,0)$ lies on $\\mathrm{Π}.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,-3,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ (\\mathbf{r}-⟨0,-3,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨3,5,0⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186363934 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,2,3⟩.$ From the Cartesian equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨0,-5,4⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=⟨0,-5,4⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨0,-5,4⟩ & =⟨0,2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-5,4⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨0,-5,4⟩ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-5,4⟩ & =2 \\\\ -5y+4z & =2 \\\\ -5y+4z-2 & =0 \\\\ 5y-4z+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186364302 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨1,1,0⟩.$ From the vectorial equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨-2,-3,1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=⟨-2,-3,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-2,-3,1⟩ & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,-3,1⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-2,-3,1⟩ & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,-3,1⟩ & =-5 \\\\ -2x-3y+z & =-5 \\\\ -2x-3y+z+5 & =0 \\\\ 2x+3y-z-5 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186364961 "The direction vectors of the lines are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=⟨7,-3,4⟩$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=⟨3,-5,1⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $E(4,-2,5)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $\\mathbf{e}=⟨4,-2,5⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{e}+s{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+t{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨4,-2,5⟩+s⟨7,-3,4⟩+t⟨3,-5,1⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186366205 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). We already have one vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$ Let's also use the displacement vector $\\overset{\\to }{CD}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =⟨-3,2,4⟩-⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $\\mathbf{c}=⟨1,1,1⟩$ of the point $C,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{c}+s\\mathbf{v}+t\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨1,1,1⟩+s⟨0,1,1⟩+t⟨-4,1,3⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186374394 "A point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3<x<6,\\hspace{2em}1<y<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we also need to\n\n- *include* the left edge ($x=3$), and\n\n- *include* the bottom edge ($y=1$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbols to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}x<6,\\hspace{2em}1\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}3\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}x\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}6,\\hspace{0.222em}1\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}4\\}.\n$$"] [17592186374407 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb{Z},y\\in \\mathbb{Z},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{Z}\\times \\mathbb{Z}={\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=x-1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<x-1\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that some points in our set lie *on* the line. We include these points by changing the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}x-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}x-1\\}\n$$"] [17592186374421 "A point $P(x,y)$ lies inside the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2<x<6,\\hspace{1em}2<y<5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}2}}<x<\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6}},\\hspace{0.167em}\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}2}}<y<\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}5}}\\}\n$$"] [17592186374453 "A point $P(x,y)$ lies inside or on the rectangle's edges if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\le x\\le 6,\\hspace{2em}3\\le y\\le 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}1}}\\le x\\le \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}6}},\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}3}}\\le y\\le \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}5}}\\}\n$$"] [17592186374485 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=4$ and vertical radius $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=64.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *outside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point outside the region, say, $(5,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n4(5)^{2}+16(0)^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}{\\color{blue}>}\\hspace{0.222em}64.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}>64.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}=64$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}64.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {\\mathbb{R}}^{2}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}4}}{x}^{2}+\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}16}}{y}^{2}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\ge }}64\\}.\n$$"] [17592186383633 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x-3$ and *below* the line $y=x+4.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x-3,\\hspace{2em}y<x+4.\n$$\n\n \n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-x-3<y<x+4.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the lines $y=-x-3$ and $y=x+4$ since the boundaries are included in the set (the lines are solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbols above as follows:\n\n$$\n-x-3\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}x+4\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in \\mathbb{R},y\\in \\mathbb{R},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}\\times \\mathbb{R}={\\mathbb{R}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{R}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}-x-3\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}\\le }}x+4\\}.\n$$"] [17592186383659 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in \\mathbb{Z},y\\in \\mathbb{Z},$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{Z}\\times \\mathbb{Z}={\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=2.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<2\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in \\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}{\\mathbb{Z}}^{2}}}\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}y\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{{\\color{blue}<}}2\\}\n$$"] [17592186387852 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨2,-1,3⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(-4,1,3)$ and $B(2,3,5)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,3,5⟩-⟨-4,1,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,2,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨6,2,2⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨6,2,2⟩ & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387873 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $\\mathrm{Π}$ is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨7,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x+y-3 & =0 \\\\ y & =3-7x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$, we get $y=3.$ Hence, the point $P(0,3,0)$ lies on $\\mathrm{Π}.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,3,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ (\\mathbf{r}-⟨0,3,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨7,1,0⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387887 "The direction vectors of the lines are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=⟨2,-2,1⟩$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=⟨3,4,7⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $A(7,-2,3)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $\\mathbf{a}=⟨7,-2,3⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{a}+s{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+t{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨7,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-2,1⟩+t⟨3,4,7⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387957 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨3,-4,2⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =⟨4,3,2⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,5,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $\\mathbf{p}=⟨1,-2,3⟩$ of the point $P,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{p}+s\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+t\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨1,-2,3⟩+s⟨2,-2,-1⟩+t⟨3,5,-1⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186387964 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =⟨3,1,-2⟩-⟨3,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =⟨5,1,2⟩-⟨3,2,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $\\mathbf{d}=⟨3,2,-1⟩$ of the point $D,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{d}+s\\overset{\\to }{DE}+t\\overset{\\to }{DF} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨3,2,-1⟩+s⟨0,-1,-1⟩+t⟨2,-1,3⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186390848 "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $\\mathbf{q}=⟨3,2,0⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{n}=⟨1,-2,5⟩$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $Q({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{q})\\cdot \\mathbf{n}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $\\mathbf{q}$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{q})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,-2,5⟩ & =⟨3,2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-2,5⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,-2,5⟩ & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186391180 "Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨-3,0,4⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2-t \\\\ y &=4+3t \\\\ z &=-6+5t\\end{aligned}\\right.\\hspace{1em}\\Rightarrow \\hspace{1em}⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨2,4,-6⟩+t\\text{ }\\underbrace{⟨-1,3,5⟩}_{\\mathbf{v}}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $\\mathbf{v}=⟨-1,3,5⟩.$ So, a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\mathbf{v}=⟨-1,3,5⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩ & =⟨-3,0,4⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-1,3,5⟩ & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186391682 "From the parametric equation, we have that $\\mathbf{v}=⟨-1,1,1⟩$ and $\\mathbf{w}=⟨0,2,2⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{v}\\times \\mathbf{w} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\enclose{left right}{\\begin{array}{ccc}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{array}} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{j}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨0,2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨0,2,-2⟩.$"] [17592186392994 "First, notice that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is normal to the plane. \n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n}=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393015 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $\\mathrm{Π}$ is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨2,1,-2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z-4 & =0 \\\\ -2z & =-2x-y+4 \\\\ z & =x+\\frac{1}{2}y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-2.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-2)$ lies on $\\mathrm{Π}.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,0,-2⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ (\\mathbf{r}-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,1,-2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393022 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $\\mathrm{Π}$ is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨0,7,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\mathrm{Π}$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y-z-13 & =0 \\\\ z & =7y-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $y=0$, we get $z=-13.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\mathrm{Π}.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨0,0,-13⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ (\\mathbf{r}-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,7,-1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393029 "We need a vector $\\mathbf{v}=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,2,-1⟩=7$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}=⟨1,2,-1⟩.$ So, $\\mathbf{v}⊥\\mathbf{n}$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{v}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,2,-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ {t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}-{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{3} & ={t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=⟨{t}_{1},\\hspace{0.167em}{t}_{2},\\hspace{0.167em}{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $\\mathbf{v}$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{2}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{2}=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=⟨0,\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{1},\\hspace{0.167em}\\bbox[Gainsboro, 2pt]{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186393036 "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $\\mathbf{q}=⟨2,1,-1⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(2,1,0)$ and $B(3,2,1)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨3,2,1⟩-⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=⟨1,1,1⟩.$ \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{q})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{q}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =⟨2,1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393043 "Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=⟨3,-5,-2⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2-3t \\\\ y &=3+4t \\\\ z &=4-t\\end{aligned}\\right.\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨2,3,4⟩+t\\text{ }\\underbrace{⟨-3,4,-1⟩}_{\\mathbf{v}}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $\\mathbf{v}=⟨-3,4,-1⟩,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\mathbf{v}=⟨-3,4,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p})\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n}-\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =0 \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} & =\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ & =⟨3,-5,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{r}\\cdot ⟨-3,4,-1⟩ & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393057 "The direction vectors of the lines are ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}=⟨-3,0,1⟩$ and ${\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}}=⟨2,1,-\\frac{1}{5}⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $H(-3,2,7)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $\\mathbf{h}=⟨-3,2,7⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{h}+s{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{1}}+t{\\mathbf{v}}_{\\mathbf{2}} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨-3,2,7⟩+s⟨-3,0,1⟩+t⟨2,1,-\\frac{1}{5}⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186393064 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨2,-9,0⟩-⟨3,6,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-15,-2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =⟨3,0,1⟩-⟨3,6,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-6,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $\\mathbf{p}=⟨3,6,2⟩$ of the point $P,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r} & =\\mathbf{p}+s\\overset{\\to }{PQ}+t\\overset{\\to }{PR} \\\\ \\mathbf{r} & =⟨3,6,2⟩+s⟨-1,-15,-2⟩+t⟨0,-6,-1⟩,\\hspace{2em}s,t\\in (-\\mathrm{∞},\\mathrm{∞}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186660905 "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\nc\\times (2a-3b)=c\\times (2a)-c\\times (3b)\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\nc\\times (2a)-c\\times (3b)=2(c\\times a)-3(c\\times b)\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $c\\times a$ and $c\\times b,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(c\\times a)-3(c\\times b) & =2⟨1,5,0⟩-3⟨-2,4,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,10,0⟩+⟨6,-12,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,-2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"]}
